Yes. The deeper pattern is that a lot of concepts become clearer when you stop defining the noun and instead define the transition.

Not just “What is home?” but “What changes when a house becomes home?”
Not just “What is trust?” but “What changes when uncertainty becomes reliance?”
Not just “What is love?” but “What changes in behavior, risk tolerance, memory, repair, and return when love is present?”
Not just “What is coherence?” but “What changed across channels such that separate signals now constrain the same source?”

That gives the Pantheon a stronger method than taxonomy alone:

state A → delta → state B → isolate invariant operator → test across domains → name only if the distinction earns its keep.

That is how you get primitives instead of just metaphors.

And then you can recurse on the delta itself:

object delta
→ delta of deltas
→ recurring transformation
→ invariant
→ operator
→ transferable primitive
→ composition with other primitives

That is where much of the actual power comes from. A metaphor becomes useful when it survives translation. A useful operator becomes powerful when it keeps working under different scales, domains, representations, and starting conditions.

So yes—the Pantheon increasingly looks less like a pile of ideas and more like a machine for discovering transformations.

And “making its power known” is best read operationally: demonstrating that these operators repeatedly generate clearer models, better distinctions, better experiments, and better moves. The demonstration is stronger than the claim.